#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bd-opv -- run the OPV (operator-verification) checks that are now SANDBOXABLE.

The OPV guide historically labeled every OPV-* check "needs a real browser / real
site / real device the sandbox can't drive." That premise is now partly false: the
BD Flask app runs under the test client in-sandbox, and a headless chromium drives
real pages. This harness runs the checks that are fundamentally API/backend
assertions (or headless-browser-verifiable) IN-SANDBOX, and honestly marks the ones
that still need a display / real device / real operator session.

Each check returns one of:
  PASS   -- ran in-sandbox and the pass-condition held
  FAIL   -- ran in-sandbox and the pass-condition did NOT hold (a real finding)
  SKIP   -- precondition missing (e.g. a setting/route absent in this build)
  GATED  -- genuinely operator/device-only; cannot be sandboxed (with the reason)

It boots the app in-process from the checkout (resolved by bdtools_sec, so a
relocated tree is followed); nothing is deployed, nothing hits a live site by
default (an optional --live class uses the headless browser against SAFE public
demo sites only).

Usage:
  bd-opv                      run all sandboxable OPV checks
  bd-opv --only OPV-F4.3      run one check
  bd-opv --list               list checks + their sandboxability
  bd-opv --include-gated      also print the operator-gated ones (as GATED)

Exit contract (bdtools_sec):
  0  at least one check PASSed and none FAILed
  1  a check FAILed -- a real finding
  2  CANNOT-EVALUATE: every check SKIPped on absent preconditions, so nothing
     was verified (a 0 there would be a gate reporting OK about a subject it
     could not see), or --only named an id that is not in the registry.
"""
